      "writtenOn": "2025-11-14T18:10:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e There shouldn\u0027t be any difference in what gets logged by Lucene between those two.\n\nThat\u0027s true, however, the `JavaLoggingInfoStream` does come with even more additional overhead for very little benefit.\n`JavaLoggingInfoStream` requires you to pass in a Logging level that will be used for all logs coming from Lucene. This means that messages coming from Lucene will only be logged when set the log level to the specified one.\n\nHowever, if we go down this route, EVERYTHING that Lucene logs will be passed onto log4j, and then log4j will need to filter them out based on log4j\u0027s own logging level adding even more load on the system, wether we\u0027re interested in logging Lucene logs or not. Log4j suggests a 6000% penalty to filter out these logs[1].\nLog4j v2 has a way around this, however we\u0027re on version v1.7.\n\nI can see some benefits, of course, i.e. being able to have extra logging from Lucene without having to restart the node, however, I\u0027m not sure if these benefits outweight the penalty cost we\u0027re always applying onto the system.\n\nI\u0027ll run some tests over the weekend and report on results.\n\n\u003e where we differentiate between Lucene index files\n\nThis can be done with the File stream too.\n\n[1] https://www.slf4j.org/legacy.html",
      "parentUuid": "4339a0d6_dad52e4a",
      "revId": "6e3735df2db87f5d2989a191bee990d701ce9ec7",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
